    if (!hasMore) return;
    const signal = controller.signal;
    fetch(url, { signal })
      .then(response => response.json())
      .then(data => {
        setData([...data, ...data]);
        setHasMore(data.length === 20); // assuming 20 items per page
      })
      .catch(error => {
        if (error.name === 'AbortError') {
          console.log('Request was cancelled');
        } else {
          console.error('Error:', error);
        }
      });
  };

  return (
    <InfiniteScroll
      dataLength={data.length}
      next={fetchMoreData}
      hasMore={hasMore}
      loader={<div>Loading...</div>}
    >
      {data.map(item => (
        <div key={item.id}>{item.name}</div>
      ))}
    </InfiniteScroll>
  );
}
```
In this example, we create a new `AbortController` instance and store it in the component's state. We then use the `signal` property of the controller to pass it to the `fetch` function.

When the component is unmounted, we call `controller.abort()` to cancel any ongoing requests.

When the user scrolls away from the component, the `fetchMoreData` function will be called again, and a new `AbortController` instance will be created. This will cancel any previous requests and start a new one.

Note that you should also handle the case where the user scrolls back to the component and the request is still ongoing. You can do this by checking if the `controller` instance is still valid before making a new request.

Here is a Rust program that finds the shortest path between two nodes in an undirected graph using Dijkstra's algorithm:
```
use std::collections::{BinaryHeap, HashMap};

struct Node {
    id: i32,
    distance: i32,
    previous: Option<i32>,
}

impl PartialEq for Node {
    fn eq(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {
        self.distance == other.distance
    }
}

impl Eq for Node {}

impl PartialOrd for Node {
    fn partial_cmp(&self, other: &Self) -> Option<std::cmp::Ordering> {
        other.distance.partial_cmp(&self.distance)
    }
}

impl Ord for Node {
    fn cmp(&self, other: &Self) -> std::cmp::Ordering {
        other.distance.cmp(&self.distance)
    }
}

fn dijkstra(graph: &HashMap<i32, Vec<(i32, i32)>>, start: i32, end: i32) -> Option<Vec<i32>> {
    let mut queue: BinaryHeap<Node> = BinaryHeap::new();
    let mut distances: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new();
    let mut previous: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new();

    distances.insert(start, 0);
    queue.push(Node { id: start, distance: 0, previous: None });

    while let Some(node) = queue.pop() {
        if node.id == end {
            let mut path = vec![end];
            let mut current = end;
            while let Some(previous_id) = previous.get(&current) {
                path.push(*previous_id);
                current = *previous_id;
            }
            path.reverse();
            return Some(path);
        }

        for &(neighbor, weight) in &graph[&node.id] {
            let distance = node.distance + weight;
            if !distances.contains_key(&neighbor) || distance < distances[&neighbor] {
                distances.insert(neighbor, distance);
                previous.insert(neighbor, node.id);
                queue.push(Node { id: neighbor, distance, previous: Some(node.id) });
            }
        }
    }

    None
}

fn main() {
    let graph = HashMap::from([
        (0, vec![(1, 4), (2, 2)]),
        (1, vec![(0, 4), (3, 5)]),
        (2, vec![(0, 2), (3, 1), (4, 8)]),
        (3, vec![(1, 5), (2, 1), (4, 3)]),
        (4, vec![(2, 8), (3, 3)]),
    ]);

    let start = 0;
    let end = 4;

    if let Some(path) = dijkstra(&graph, start, end) {
        println!("Shortest path from {} to {}: {:?}", start, end, path);
    } else {
        println!("No path from {} to {}", start, end);
    }
}
```
This program defines a `Node` struct to represent a node in the graph, with an `id`, `distance` from the start node, and `previous` node in the shortest path. The `dijkstra` function implements Dijkstra's algorithm, using a priority queue to keep track of nodes to visit, and two hash maps to store the shortest distances and previous nodes in the shortest path. The `main` function creates a sample graph and finds the shortest path from node 0 to node 4.

Note that this implementation assumes that the graph is undirected and connected, and that the weights are non-negative. If your graph has negative weights or is directed, you may need to modify the implementation accordingly.

Here is an expression in JavaScript to reverse the elements of an array without using the built-in `reverse()` method or any other array manipulation methods, with a time complexity of O(n):
```
let arr = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
let reversedArr = [];
for (let i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
  reversedArr[reversedArr.length] = arr[i];
}
console.log(reversedArr); // [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]
```
Here's how it works:

1. We create an empty array `reversedArr` that will store the reversed elements.
2. We iterate over the original array `arr` in reverse order using a `for` loop, starting from the last index (`arr.length - 1`) and decrementing the index `i` until it reaches 0.
3. In each iteration, we add the current element `arr[i]` to the end of the `reversedArr` using the syntax `reversedArr[reversedArr.length] = arr[i]`. This effectively appends the element to the end of the array.
4. After the loop completes, `reversedArr` contains the reversed elements of the original array.

This solution has a time complexity of O(n) because we only iterate over the array once, and each iteration takes constant time.

